Crane Control Specification

This project defines software and hardware specifications for the control of a crane system.
The element controlled is the Payload Winch which raises and lowers the payload of the crane system.

Hardware
    Arduino Pro Mini 5v 16Mhz
    ULSwitch  - Digital Input, Upper Limit Switch - active Low Hall Effect Sensor
    KeyPhasor - Digital Input, Winch rotation     - active Low Hall Effect Sensor
    Winch     - Servo Output                      - continupus rotation winch servo
    Lights    - Digital Output, crane lights      - active Low LED Control
    SDA       - I2C Data Line                     - see separate note on I2C interface
    SCL       - I2C Clock Line                    - see separate note on I2C interface
    Servo0v   - Digital Output, servo power       - active High output to supply servo power after initialisation

Electrical
    Electrical connection to the crane is via a 4 wire slip ring
    +5v
    0v
    SDA (I2C data line)
    SCL (I2C clock line)


Servo Control
    The Servo Control section manages the power and operation of the Winch servo.
    The Servo has three preset (defined) demands; Stop, Raise, and Lower.
    
I2C Interface
    The I2C interface is used for communication between the crane control system and other I2C-enabled devices.
    The SDA line carries the data, and the SCL line carries the clock signal.
    Both lines require pull-up resistors to function correctly.

    The I2C interface carries a command word from the master device to control the crane system and provides a status word back to the master device.

    The command word can take the following values:
   
        0x00    Disable all movement (this is the default powerup value)
        0x01    Move Winch Payload to Position 1
        0x02    Move Winch Payload to Position 2
        0x03    Move Winch Payload to Position 3
        0x04    Move Winch Payload to Upper Limit

    The status word returned by the crane system can take the following values:
        0x00    Winch Movement Disabled
        0x01    Winch Payload at Position 1
        0x02    Winch Payload at Position 2
        0x03    Winch Payload at Position 3
        0x04    Winch Payload at Upper Limit
        0x05    Winch Payload Moving
        0x06    Not initialised
        0x07    Error Condition

Control Logic - PowerUp
    Enable - throughout PowerUp periodically check the state of the I2C interface and suspend operation if Disable all movement is demanded, update the status of the I2C status word accordingly
    
    Set I2C Status to 'Not initialised' (0x04)

    Servo initialisation
        Set servo demand to 'Stop'
        Activate the Servo0v output to supply power to the Winch servo

    Set the Lights to Pattern 0

    Raise Winch payload
        The Winch must be raised (servo demand set to Raise) until the ULSwitch indicates that the payload is fully raised
        A pulseCount should be maintained of the KeyPhasor pulses and if no pulses are detected within a certain timeframe then this is an error (error 1)
        Once at the Upper Limit
            Clear the pulseCount (used to record the payload position)
            Set the Lights to Pattern 1
            Set I2C Status to 'Winch Payload at Upper Limit' (0x04)
            Move to the Main Loop

Control Logic - Main Loop

    Control the Winch
        Monitor I2C demand word
            If the derived position of the winch payload (the pulseCount) differs from the I2C demand then activate the Winch to move the payload to the demanded position
            
            If demand is 'Move to Position n' or 'Lower to Home'
                Activate the Winch Servo to move the payload in the appropriate direction and track the pulseCount until it reaches the desired position (as determined by the KeyPhasor count)

                Stop the Winch Servo, update the I2C Status to reflect the new position 

Control Logic - Lights
    At PowerUp the lights are turned off

    Following successful initialisation, the lights will operate according to the preset blink rate and brightness levels (pattern 0).
        The Lights have two brightness levels: low and high, controlled by preset PWM duty cycles.
        There is an overall blink rate set to 0.2 Hz; with the brightness set to Low for 95% of the time and High for 5% of the time.
    
    If an error occurs (see Error 1) the lights will flash in a specific error pattern with a 50% duty cycle.

